1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:01,600
The preview does support importing

2
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:03,660
existing definitions using standard

3
00:00:03,660 --> 00:00:06,220
semantic web formats like Turtle, RDF,

4
00:00:06,420 --> 00:00:07,180
and O -W -L.

5
00:00:07,439 --> 00:00:08,080
Briefly, what are those?

6
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:09,880
Those are essentially the legacy standard

7
00:00:09,880 --> 00:00:11,720
coding languages that researchers and

8
00:00:11,720 --> 00:00:13,140
data scientists have used for years

9
00:00:13,140 --> 00:00:14,460
to write out these kinds of

10
00:00:14,460 --> 00:00:16,360
complex knowledge webs, originally

11
00:00:16,360 --> 00:00:17,860
designed for the semantic web.

12
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:19,400
So you can just import them.

13
00:00:19,900 --> 00:00:21,320
However, the Microsoft documentation

14
00:00:21,320 --> 00:00:23,580
explicitly warns that some elements

15
00:00:23,580 --> 00:00:26,280
might be adjusted, modified, or entirely

16
00:00:26,280 --> 00:00:28,220
skipped during the import process.

17
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:31,080
Full lossless translation of complex

18
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:33,239
OWL behaviors is not promised.

19
00:00:33,519 --> 00:00:34,339
You have to check it.

20
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:36,160
You have to thoroughly and manually

21
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:37,879
review the import results to ensure

22
00:00:37,879 --> 00:00:39,579
your logic survived the translation.
